1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,296
It is nine thirty-one in the morning, June eighteenth, twenty twenty-three.

2
00:00:04,296 --> 00:00:10,782
A submersible the size of a minivan slips beneath the North Atlantic, carrying five people toward the Titanic.

3
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:19,187
For an hour and forty-five minutes, it checks in with the surface every fifteen minutes, exactly as planned.

4
00:00:19,187 --> 00:00:21,617
Then, at ten forty-seven, it stops.

5
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:27,821
For four days, the world will watch a countdown clock. Ninety-six hours of oxygen.

6
00:00:27,821 --> 00:00:32,875
Banging sounds on sonar. A search across thousands of square miles of open ocean.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,186
But a secret Navy listening system had already heard the truth, on day one.

8
00:00:38,186 --> 00:00:42,672
They were gone within milliseconds of losing contact. The world just didn't know it yet.

9
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,698
This was not a mystery.

10
00:00:45,698 --> 00:00:52,911
For five years, engineers, employees, and experts had warned Stockton Rush, in writing, that his submarine could kill someone.

11
00:00:52,911 --> 00:00:55,189
He ignored every one of them.

12
00:00:56,333 --> 00:00:57,900
You're watching mindwired.

13
00:00:59,033 --> 00:00:59,869
OceanGate.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,769
Chapter one. The man who broke the rules.

15
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:09,079
To understand how this happened, you have to start with the man behind it.

16
00:01:09,079 --> 00:01:12,363
And he was not a con artist chasing a quick payday.

17
00:01:12,363 --> 00:01:15,349
He genuinely believed he was the future of ocean exploration.

18
00:01:16,466 --> 00:01:26,837
As a teenager, he became one of the youngest people in the world to earn a jet transport rating, qualifying to fly a DC-eight airliner before most kids his age had a driver's license.

19
00:01:27,966 --> 00:01:35,222
Richard Stockton Rush the Third studied aerospace engineering at Princeton, then became a flight-test engineer on the F-fifteen fighter program.

20
00:01:35,222 --> 00:01:37,762
He came from money, and from history.

21
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:47,651
By his own account, he had always wanted to be an astronaut, but by the time he was old enough, the golden age of piloted space exploration had already passed him by.

22
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:56,619
So he looked for the next frontier, and found it in the one place on Earth as unexplored, and as unforgiving, as outer space.

23
00:01:56,619 --> 00:02:00,529
The deep ocean. Ninety-five percent of it, still unseen by human eyes.

24
00:02:01,666 --> 00:02:05,489
He carried the bloodline of men who signed the Declaration of Independence.

25
00:02:05,489 --> 00:02:09,630
His wife Wendy descended from a couple who died aboard the original Titanic.

26
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:13,134
A coincidence that would come to feel, later, like a warning.

27
00:02:14,266 --> 00:02:21,188
In two thousand nine, Rush and a partner named Guillermo Söhnlein founded OceanGate out of Everett, Washington.

28
00:02:21,188 --> 00:02:26,073
The pitch was simple: manned submersibles for science, industry, and paying explorers.

29
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:34,166
Their earlier submersibles, Antipodes and then Cyclops One, proved the company could actually put people underwater and bring them back.

30
00:02:34,166 --> 00:02:37,649
This was not two guys with a website. It worked.

31
00:02:38,766 --> 00:02:43,166
So Rush set his sights on the ultimate prize. Not a shallow research dive.

32
00:02:43,166 --> 00:02:49,137
The Titanic herself, resting two and a half miles down, in one of the most hostile environments on Earth.

33
00:02:50,266 --> 00:02:59,708
To get there, he needed a hull that could survive crushing pressure, six thousand pounds per square inch, while staying light enough to be affordable and repeatable.

34
00:02:59,708 --> 00:03:01,107
His answer: carbon fiber.

35
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:09,292
Titan's pressure hull was a carbon-fiber cylinder about five inches thick, capped with titanium on both ends.

36
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:14,275
Lighter than steel. Cheaper to build. And, Rush argued, just as strong.

37
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:20,854
No one had ever built a crewed submersible with a carbon-fiber pressure hull rated for the Titanic's depth.

38
00:03:20,854 --> 00:03:24,491
Rush wasn't troubled by that. He saw it as exactly the point.

39
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:29,928
The traditional path for a vessel like this is called classing.

40
00:03:29,928 --> 00:03:40,471
An independent marine society, like DNV or the American Bureau of Shipping, inspects every weld, every material, every system, before a single paying customer ever climbs aboard.

41
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:45,622
Rush called that process slow, bureaucratic, and hostile to genuine innovation.

42
00:03:45,622 --> 00:03:50,743
He believed his own engineering judgment, and his sensors, were a faster, better substitute.

43
00:03:50,743 --> 00:03:54,765
He would spend the rest of his life defending that bet.

44
00:03:55,900 --> 00:04:06,662
His signature safety claim was a real-time hull-health monitoring system: acoustic sensors and strain gauges built into the hull, listening for the carbon fiber beginning to fail, before it was too late.

45
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:11,541
And he steered it with something almost comically ordinary.

46
00:04:11,541 --> 00:04:13,204
A store-bought video-game controller.

47
00:04:13,204 --> 00:04:17,361
A detail he showed off, on camera, with obvious pride.

48
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:24,111
Rush did not hide his philosophy. He said it, on camera, again and again.

49
00:04:24,111 --> 00:04:31,326
"At some point, safety just is pure waste." He called the entire commercial submarine industry, quote, "obscenely safe."

50
00:04:32,466 --> 00:04:41,571
His motto, more than once: "You're remembered for the rules you break." On combining carbon fiber and titanium in a single hull, he put it even more bluntly.

51
00:04:41,571 --> 00:04:44,822
"There's a rule you don't do that. Well, I did."

52
00:04:45,966 --> 00:04:53,350
To lend the project weight, OceanGate publicly named Boeing, NASA, and the University of Washington as partners in Titan's design.

53
00:04:53,350 --> 00:04:57,042
It sounded like the pedigree of a serious aerospace program.

54
00:04:58,166 --> 00:05:03,409
After the disaster, all three pushed back. Boeing said it never designed or built the sub.

55
00:05:03,409 --> 00:05:06,030
NASA said it only consulted, and approved nothing.

56
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:09,634
The university said its lab was not involved in Titan's design.

57
00:05:10,766 --> 00:05:16,290
And here is the part that made all of this believable. It worked.

58
00:05:16,290 --> 00:05:22,239
In twenty twenty-one and twenty twenty-two, Titan actually reached the Titanic, and came back.

59
00:05:22,239 --> 00:05:23,514
Again, and again.

60
00:05:24,633 --> 00:05:28,194
Passengers paid around two hundred fifty thousand dollars a seat.

61
00:05:28,194 --> 00:05:31,755
They came away calling it the experience of a lifetime.

62
00:05:31,755 --> 00:05:35,317
Stockton Rush looked, for a moment, like a genuine pioneer.

63
00:05:36,466 --> 00:05:43,363
For a brief moment, OceanGate had beaten the odds that a hundred years of maritime skeptics said it couldn't beat.

64
00:05:43,363 --> 00:05:50,259
A carbon-fiber submarine, ferrying tourists past the most famous wreck in human history, and bringing every one of them home.

65
00:05:51,399 --> 00:05:55,756
But even in those successful years, the cracks were already showing.

66
00:05:55,756 --> 00:06:00,904
Of roughly ninety total dives, only thirteen ever reached the Titanic's full depth.

67
00:06:00,904 --> 00:06:03,676
And the warnings had never actually stopped.

68
00:06:04,799 --> 00:06:10,133
By late twenty twenty-two, Rush was a minor celebrity. CBS Sunday Morning profiled him.

69
00:06:10,133 --> 00:06:12,418
Journalists lined up for a seat.

70
00:06:12,418 --> 00:06:18,513
He appeared, again and again, as the face of a new golden age of ocean exploration.

71
00:06:19,633 --> 00:06:26,268
He talked openly about expanding the fleet, about eventually offering trips to other deep wrecks, other frontiers entirely.

72
00:06:26,268 --> 00:06:29,586
In his own mind, Titan was only the beginning.

73
00:06:30,733 --> 00:06:33,737
Chapter two. The man who tried to stop him.

74
00:06:34,866 --> 00:06:36,695
His name was David Lochridge.

75
00:06:36,695 --> 00:06:45,472
OceanGate's Director of Marine Operations, a veteran submersible pilot whose entire job was making sure the crew and the paying passengers came home alive.

76
00:06:46,599 --> 00:06:51,825
In January twenty eighteen, Lochridge delivered a quality inspection report to OceanGate's leadership.

77
00:06:51,825 --> 00:06:53,835
What he found terrified him.

78
00:06:54,966 --> 00:06:56,373
This is his report.

79
00:06:56,373 --> 00:07:05,514
It warned that the forward viewport, the porthole the passengers would look through, was certified by its own manufacturer to just one thousand three hundred meters.

80
00:07:05,514 --> 00:07:07,975
OceanGate intended to dive to four thousand.

81
00:07:09,099 --> 00:07:10,458
His report went further.

82
00:07:10,458 --> 00:07:17,928
He alleged the carbon-fiber hull had never received the non-destructive testing that could catch a hidden flaw before it became a catastrophe.

83
00:07:19,066 --> 00:07:22,429
And the acoustic monitoring system Rush was so proud of?

84
00:07:22,429 --> 00:07:29,828
Lochridge's report warned it would only detect a failure once it had already begun, in his words, often milliseconds before an implosion.

85
00:07:30,966 --> 00:07:32,658
He was fired that same month.

86
00:07:32,658 --> 00:07:37,732
His counterclaim later said he was given about ten minutes to clear his desk and leave the building.

87
00:07:38,866 --> 00:07:42,486
Then OceanGate sued him. For breach of contract.

88
00:07:42,486 --> 00:07:47,916
For disclosing trade secrets, including, they alleged, to federal workplace safety regulators.

89
00:07:47,916 --> 00:07:52,894
Lochridge counter-sued, saying he had been fired for being a whistleblower.

90
00:07:54,033 --> 00:07:58,343
The case settled quietly, out of court, in late twenty eighteen.

91
00:07:58,343 --> 00:08:03,437
The terms were never made public. And inside OceanGate, everyone understood the lesson.

92
00:08:04,566 --> 00:08:12,256
OceanGate's own position, in writing, was that submitting Titan to independent certification was, quote, anathema to rapid innovation.

93
00:08:12,256 --> 00:08:17,810
Every marine classification society in the world, they said, was simply too slow.

94
00:08:18,933 --> 00:08:24,749
It is worth sitting with that argument for a moment, because it is not entirely crazy.

95
00:08:24,749 --> 00:08:28,021
Classification can genuinely be slow. Regulation can genuinely calcify.

96
00:08:28,021 --> 00:08:32,020
Rush was not wrong that innovation sometimes needs room to move.

97
00:08:33,166 --> 00:08:36,899
But there is a difference between moving fast and refusing to look.

98
00:08:36,899 --> 00:08:43,432
And every person who actually inspected Titan's hull up close came away asking Rush to slow down, not to speed up.

99
00:08:44,566 --> 00:08:47,773
OceanGate's blog post laid out its philosophy in full.

100
00:08:47,773 --> 00:08:57,392
Bringing outside engineers up to speed on every innovation before real-world testing, it argued, would smother the entire project in bureaucracy before it ever left the dock.

101
00:08:58,533 --> 00:09:02,964
It is a seductive argument, especially to an engineer who believes his own data.

102
00:09:02,964 --> 00:09:09,295
But data from one team, checked only by that same team, is not the same thing as an independent verdict.

103
00:09:10,433 --> 00:09:13,672
Chapter three. The industry that begged him to stop.

104
00:09:14,799 --> 00:09:16,227
Lochridge was not alone.

105
00:09:16,227 --> 00:09:21,225
In March of twenty eighteen, an entire industry organization put its warning in writing.

106
00:09:22,366 --> 00:09:29,581
The Marine Technology Society sent Stockton Rush this letter, signed by dozens of the world's leading submersible experts.

107
00:09:29,581 --> 00:09:36,394
It warned that his experimental approach could lead to outcomes, in their words, from minor to catastrophic.

108
00:09:37,533 --> 00:09:45,584
The letter called OceanGate's marketing misleading to the public, for advertising Titan as meeting or exceeding safety standards it never actually intended to follow.

109
00:09:45,584 --> 00:09:50,281
It begged him, one more time, to let an outside body certify the sub.

110
00:09:51,399 --> 00:09:56,277
The letter was written by Will Kohnen, who chaired the society's committee on manned submersibles.

111
00:09:56,277 --> 00:09:59,854
This was not a rival company trying to slow him down.

112
00:09:59,854 --> 00:10:03,755
This was the closest thing the industry had to a unanimous consensus.

113
00:10:04,899 --> 00:10:06,355
Rush's answer never changed.

114
00:10:06,355 --> 00:10:11,815
He called warnings like this baseless cries of, quote, you are going to kill someone.

115
00:10:11,815 --> 00:10:15,819
He said he'd heard them, in his words, way too often.

116
00:10:16,966 --> 00:10:21,721
Expedition specialist Rob McCallum warned him directly, by email, that same year.

117
00:10:21,721 --> 00:10:26,079
You are potentially putting an entire industry at risk, he wrote.

118
00:10:26,079 --> 00:10:28,852
Rush's lawyers reportedly threatened him into silence.

119
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,615
Here is why the engineers were so afraid. Carbon fiber is superb in tension.

120
00:10:35,615 --> 00:10:38,021
It holds up a jet's fuselage.

121
00:10:38,021 --> 00:10:42,433
But under crushing pressure, dive after dive, it behaves completely differently.

122
00:10:43,566 --> 00:10:46,178
Each descent is a compression cycle.

123
00:10:46,178 --> 00:10:54,015
Microscopic layers begin to separate, a process called delamination, and the damage builds silently, invisibly, dive after dive.

124
00:10:55,133 --> 00:10:59,286
A steel or titanium sphere deforms before it fails. It warns you.

125
00:10:59,286 --> 00:11:06,209
A carbon-fiber hull can hold, and hold, and hold, and then fail all at once, with no warning at all.

126
00:11:07,333 --> 00:11:12,969
In twenty nineteen, submersible expert Karl Stanley rode Titan on a deep test dive off the Bahamas.

127
00:11:12,969 --> 00:11:14,959
He heard a loud cracking sound.

128
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:20,264
He emailed Rush that it sounded like an area of the hull breaking down, getting spongy.

129
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:26,440
OceanGate did cancel the rest of that season's dives, and rebuilt part of the hull.

130
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:30,809
For a while, it looked like the warning had finally landed. It hadn't.

131
00:11:30,809 --> 00:11:33,834
It was a pause, not a change of course.

132
00:11:34,966 --> 00:11:38,120
He kept emailing warnings for over a year.

133
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:47,975
Titan operated in international waters, beyond any nation's inspection rules, and every passenger who boarded was labeled, on paper, a mission specialist, not a passenger.

134
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:54,160
That label was exactly what kept Titan outside the inspections meant to protect paying customers.

135
00:11:54,160 --> 00:12:00,907
James Cameron, who has dived to the Titanic dozens of times, would later call the whole thing a terrible irony.

136
00:12:02,033 --> 00:12:10,073
Cameron said the deep-sea engineering community had been very concerned, and had written letters saying plainly that Titan was too experimental to carry passengers.

137
00:12:10,073 --> 00:12:11,749
These were not internet critics.

138
00:12:11,749 --> 00:12:15,434
These were the people who had actually built submersibles that worked.

139
00:12:16,566 --> 00:12:21,023
And then Cameron drew the parallel that would haunt this entire story.

140
00:12:21,023 --> 00:12:26,595
The original Titanic's captain was warned about ice, again and again, and steamed ahead anyway.

141
00:12:26,595 --> 00:12:31,795
A century later, at the very same wreck, warnings were ignored all over again.

142
00:12:32,933 --> 00:12:36,869
None of these warnings came from a rival trying to steal business.

143
00:12:36,869 --> 00:12:44,740
They came from the exact community Rush claimed to be serving: people who loved deep-sea exploration, and wanted it to survive its own ambitions.

144
00:12:45,866 --> 00:12:48,139
Chapter four. The final dive.

145
00:12:49,266 --> 00:12:52,584
By twenty twenty-three, Titan had already completed dozens of dives.

146
00:12:52,584 --> 00:12:59,219
Nobody outside the company knew how many times the hull had been quietly pushed past what it could safely bear.

147
00:13:00,366 --> 00:13:09,535
On the morning of June eighteenth, Titan was launched from its support ship, the Polar Prince, some three hundred seventy miles off Newfoundland, directly above the Titanic.

148
00:13:10,666 --> 00:13:15,451
Aboard were five people. Stockton Rush, sixty-one, at the controls.

149
00:13:15,451 --> 00:13:19,757
Hamish Harding, fifty-eight, a British adventurer and aviation businessman.

150
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:28,606
Paul-Henri Nargeolet, seventy-seven, the French explorer known as Mister Titanic, who had spent more time at the wreck than anyone alive.

151
00:13:29,733 --> 00:13:34,939
And Shahzada Dawood, forty-eight, a British-Pakistani businessman, seated beside his son.

152
00:13:34,939 --> 00:13:37,779
Suleman Dawood. Just nineteen years old.

153
00:13:38,900 --> 00:13:41,162
The dive began just after nine-thirty.

154
00:13:41,162 --> 00:13:46,063
For the first hour and forty-five minutes, everything went exactly according to plan.

155
00:13:46,063 --> 00:13:50,211
Titan checked in with the surface every fifteen minutes, as always.

156
00:13:51,333 --> 00:13:54,650
Then, at ten forty-seven, the messages stopped.

157
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:57,608
Titan never came back up.

158
00:13:57,608 --> 00:14:03,033
After hours of silence, the crew of the Polar Prince alerted the authorities that evening.

159
00:14:03,033 --> 00:14:05,204
The submersible was overdue, and missing.

160
00:14:06,333 --> 00:14:10,606
What followed was a frantic, four-day international search. The U.S.

161
00:14:10,606 --> 00:14:18,297
Coast Guard, Canadian ships and aircraft, and deep-sea robots, scouring thousands of square miles of the North Atlantic.

162
00:14:19,433 --> 00:14:22,254
News networks around the world cut to live coverage.

163
00:14:22,254 --> 00:14:28,837
Ordinary people who had never heard of OceanGate found themselves refreshing their phones, hoping for an update, hoping for a miracle.

164
00:14:29,966 --> 00:14:33,958
Remotely operated vehicles were flown in from as far away as Europe.

165
00:14:33,958 --> 00:14:38,616
Military aircraft dropped sonar buoys across an area larger than the state of Connecticut.

166
00:14:38,616 --> 00:14:41,277
It was, by any measure, an extraordinary response.

167
00:14:42,399 --> 00:14:44,356
Then came a flicker of hope.

168
00:14:44,356 --> 00:14:47,617
Sonar buoys picked up banging sounds, roughly every thirty minutes.

169
00:14:47,617 --> 00:14:52,508
A later Navy analysis found the noises had nothing to do with Titan at all.

170
00:14:53,633 --> 00:14:56,225
And a clock hung over everything.

171
00:14:56,225 --> 00:15:00,545
Officials estimated Titan carried about ninety-six hours of breathable oxygen.

172
00:15:00,545 --> 00:15:04,866
A countdown the entire world watched, together, in real time.

173
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:11,093
On June twenty-second, a robot on the ocean floor found what searchers had feared most.

174
00:15:11,093 --> 00:15:15,168
A debris field, about sixteen hundred feet from the Titanic's own bow.

175
00:15:16,299 --> 00:15:21,494
The Coast Guard said it was consistent with a catastrophic implosion of the pressure hull.

176
00:15:21,494 --> 00:15:23,226
All five were presumed dead.

177
00:15:23,226 --> 00:15:28,421
At that depth, an implosion happens in milliseconds, faster than the human brain can register.

178
00:15:29,566 --> 00:15:31,739
And then came the hardest part.

179
00:15:31,739 --> 00:15:37,897
Officials later revealed that on June eighteenth, right around the moment contact was lost, a secret U.S.

180
00:15:37,897 --> 00:15:42,967
Navy listening system had already detected an anomaly it called consistent with an implosion.

181
00:15:44,099 --> 00:15:46,766
It wasn't considered definitive, so the search went on.

182
00:15:46,766 --> 00:15:53,581
But it meant the five men had most likely died within the first two hours, while the world spent four more days hoping.

183
00:15:54,699 --> 00:16:04,652
In the weeks that followed, the Coast Guard recovered debris from the sea floor, and, it said, presumed human remains, carefully brought up for analysis.

184
00:16:05,799 --> 00:16:08,568
Chapter five. What the investigators found.

185
00:16:09,699 --> 00:16:15,262
By July sixth, twenty twenty-three, OceanGate had suspended all exploration and commercial operations.

186
00:16:15,262 --> 00:16:18,685
The real reckoning would take two more years.

187
00:16:19,833 --> 00:16:22,720
For over a year, the story went quiet.

188
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:33,547
No hearings, no headlines, just a slow, careful reconstruction of a hull that no longer existed in one piece, and a company that had stopped talking to the press entirely.

189
00:16:34,666 --> 00:16:40,209
The Coast Guard convened a Marine Board of Investigation, its highest level of inquiry.

190
00:16:40,209 --> 00:16:47,335
For two weeks in September twenty twenty-four, in North Charleston, South Carolina, it heard sworn testimony in public.

191
00:16:48,466 --> 00:16:50,774
OceanGate's own former engineering director testified first.

192
00:16:50,774 --> 00:16:58,027
He said he felt pressured to make the sub dive-ready, and that he told Stockton Rush plainly, I'm not getting in it.

193
00:16:59,166 --> 00:17:00,547
David Lochridge testified too.

194
00:17:00,547 --> 00:17:05,723
Asked how the company had responded to every warning he'd raised, his answer was blunt.

195
00:17:05,723 --> 00:17:10,555
Everything I raised in that report, they had an excuse. It was a disgrace.

196
00:17:11,699 --> 00:17:15,347
One former technician told investigators he'd quit over the mission-specialist label.

197
00:17:15,347 --> 00:17:24,630
He said when he raised it, Rush's answer was that if the Coast Guard became a problem, he'd, quote, buy himself a congressman and make it go away.

198
00:17:25,766 --> 00:17:31,075
The hearings surfaced the moment investigators now believe was the beginning of the end.

199
00:17:31,075 --> 00:17:36,004
Dive eighty, in July twenty twenty-two. The people aboard heard a loud bang.

200
00:17:36,004 --> 00:17:38,279
Two separate sensor systems recorded it.

201
00:17:39,399 --> 00:17:44,656
The Coast Guard's own report is blunt about what that acoustic monitoring system actually was.

202
00:17:44,656 --> 00:17:48,510
Not a real-time safety alert, capable of stopping a dive mid-descent.

203
00:17:48,510 --> 00:17:53,767
A historical record, reviewed afterward, to decide whether the next dive was worth the risk.

204
00:17:54,899 --> 00:18:06,210
One of OceanGate's own project engineers put it plainly to investigators: the monitoring system should only ever have been used as a planning tool, never marketed as a real-time safety net for the people inside.

205
00:18:07,333 --> 00:18:12,977
Investigators now believe that was the sound of the hull's carbon fiber coming apart.

206
00:18:12,977 --> 00:18:18,621
An NTSB engineer told the panel the recovered hull was riddled with manufacturing defects.

207
00:18:18,621 --> 00:18:22,249
Wrinkles. Porosity. Voids, baked into the carbon fiber itself.

208
00:18:23,366 --> 00:18:29,327
In August twenty twenty-five, two years after the implosion, the Coast Guard released its final report.

209
00:18:29,327 --> 00:18:30,817
Three hundred thirty-five pages.

210
00:18:30,817 --> 00:18:34,915
Its conclusion, in the words of the board's chair, was blunt.

211
00:18:34,915 --> 00:18:39,013
This marine casualty and the loss of five lives was preventable.

212
00:18:40,133 --> 00:18:54,814
The report found OceanGate's own inadequate design, certification, and maintenance process was the primary cause, wrapped around what investigators called a toxic workplace culture, and a regulatory framework with gaps wide enough to drive a submarine through.

213
00:18:55,933 --> 00:18:57,474
The report went further still.

214
00:18:57,474 --> 00:19:03,639
Had Stockton Rush survived, the Coast Guard says it would have referred him to the Justice Department for criminal investigation.

215
00:19:04,766 --> 00:19:12,408
Two months later, in October twenty twenty-five, the NTSB released its own report, this one on exactly how the hull failed.

216
00:19:12,408 --> 00:19:20,413
It concluded the hull was damaged after dive eighty, and got progressively worse until, on its final dive, it buckled, and imploded.

217
00:19:21,533 --> 00:19:25,397
And the company, the NTSB found, never properly tested Titan's hull.

218
00:19:25,397 --> 00:19:31,721
It never actually knew how weak the submersible really was, until the moment it wasn't strong enough anymore.

219
00:19:32,866 --> 00:19:35,657
It is a strange kind of justice.

220
00:19:35,657 --> 00:19:46,423
Two federal investigations, each running for over a year, each combing through emails, testimony, and wreckage, arriving at nearly the same verdict from two completely different directions.

221
00:19:47,566 --> 00:19:59,608
In twenty twenty-four, the family of Paul-Henri Nargeolet sued for more than fifty million dollars, accusing the company of gross negligence, and of hiding the sub's flaws from the man they had talked into boarding it.

222
00:20:00,733 --> 00:20:05,459
There is a strange detail that surfaces again and again in the testimony.

223
00:20:05,459 --> 00:20:13,820
Multiple employees said Rush treated concerns not as engineering problems to be solved, but as personal challenges to his own competence and vision.

224
00:20:14,966 --> 00:20:20,689
That is, in the end, the simplest explanation for why so many warnings changed so little.

225
00:20:20,689 --> 00:20:22,835
Not because the warnings were unclear.

226
00:20:22,835 --> 00:20:28,915
Because the man receiving them had built his entire identity around being the exception to every rule.

227
00:20:30,033 --> 00:20:31,992
Chapter six. What it cost.

228
00:20:33,133 --> 00:20:36,663
The Dawoods were, by every account, not thrill-seekers chasing a stunt.

229
00:20:36,663 --> 00:20:46,612
Suleman had reportedly been nervous about the dive, and had brought along a Rubik's Cube, hoping to solve it at the bottom of the ocean, and set a small personal record.

230
00:20:47,733 --> 00:20:52,190
Paul-Henri Nargeolet had dived to the Titanic more than thirty times across three decades.

231
00:20:52,190 --> 00:20:56,329
If anyone alive understood the risks of that wreck site, it was him.

232
00:20:56,329 --> 00:20:57,921
He trusted the sub anyway.

233
00:20:59,066 --> 00:21:01,903
Both investigations reached the same underlying warning.

234
00:21:01,903 --> 00:21:08,386
The rules governing experimental submersibles like Titan were simply not built for a vehicle like this.

235
00:21:08,386 --> 00:21:14,870
The NTSB said existing regulations had, in effect, enabled OceanGate to operate the way it did.

236
00:21:15,999 --> 00:21:22,529
There is a broader question sitting underneath all of this, one both reports raised without fully answering.

237
00:21:22,529 --> 00:21:30,210
Who, exactly, is supposed to say no to a wealthy, confident founder, when the entire regulatory system assumes good faith?

238
00:21:31,333 --> 00:21:34,308
International waters have no single police force.

239
00:21:34,308 --> 00:21:36,433
Marine classification societies are voluntary.

240
00:21:36,433 --> 00:21:41,959
Passenger-vessel rules only apply to passengers, and OceanGate simply stopped calling them that.

241
00:21:41,959 --> 00:21:47,059
Every gap here was legal. That is what made it so dangerous.

242
00:21:48,199 --> 00:21:52,323
It is tempting to make this a story about one reckless man.

243
00:21:52,323 --> 00:21:56,103
But every warning came from someone who wanted OceanGate to succeed.

244
00:21:56,103 --> 00:22:00,226
Lochridge wanted it to succeed safely. So did the Marine Technology Society.

245
00:22:00,226 --> 00:22:01,600
So did Karl Stanley.

246
00:22:02,733 --> 00:22:06,111
The tragedy is not that no one saw this coming.

247
00:22:06,111 --> 00:22:12,529
The tragedy is that everyone did, and said so, in writing, for five straight years, and it changed nothing.

248
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:18,389
Five people trusted a system that had already failed them long before June eighteenth.

249
00:22:18,389 --> 00:22:20,413
Not the ocean. Not bad luck.

250
00:22:20,413 --> 00:22:25,473
A company that mistook every warning for an insult, and every rule for an obstacle.

251
00:22:26,599 --> 00:22:30,387
What stays with me about this one is the letter.

252
00:22:30,387 --> 00:22:35,311
Thirty-some experts, in writing, five years before the implosion, using the word catastrophic.

253
00:22:35,311 --> 00:22:38,720
Not as a guess. As a documented, professional warning.

254
00:22:39,866 --> 00:22:44,467
The deep ocean does not care how confident you are. It does not negotiate.

255
00:22:44,467 --> 00:22:50,054
It simply enforces its own laws, exactly as it always has, and exactly as it always will.

256
00:22:51,199 --> 00:22:54,961
Somewhere in this story is a real, human tension worth sitting with.

257
00:22:54,961 --> 00:22:58,409
We need people willing to break rules to push exploration forward.

258
00:22:58,409 --> 00:23:04,992
But we also need the people who beg them to slow down, and this time, we need them to actually listen.

259
00:23:06,133 --> 00:23:11,491
Every single warning in this story exists in writing. A report. A letter. An email.

260
00:23:11,491 --> 00:23:15,778
A hearing transcript. Nothing here is a guess made after the fact.

261
00:23:15,778 --> 00:23:20,422
It was all said, clearly, while there was still time to change course.

262
00:23:21,566 --> 00:23:32,456
If a single ignored letter could end five lives at the bottom of the ocean, wait until you see how far people were willing to go to sell two hundred thousand people a one-way ticket to Mars.

263
00:23:32,456 --> 00:23:34,810
Watch our film on the Mars One scam.

264
00:23:35,933 --> 00:23:38,331
If this story moved you, subscribe to mindwired.

265
00:23:38,331 --> 00:23:46,123
We tell the true stories of how far people will go chasing the edge of what's possible, and what it costs the people who trust them.

266
00:23:46,123 --> 00:23:47,322
This has been OceanGate.

